t if after allocating all extra capacities, remaining > 0? That would mean budget exceeds sum of capacities, but the problem likely ensures feasibility. If not, we might need to allocate as much as possible but then we can't reach budget exactly. The problem says \"exact token budget\", so we assume it's feasible. To be safe, we could allocate up to capacities and then stop; if remaining > 0, maybe it's impossible, but we'll just leave it.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Use Python standard library only.\n- Must not mutate case.\n- Return a JSON-serializable object.\n\nWe'll write the solve function.\n\nLet's produce the JSON output with key \"implementation\" and value the Python source code. Ensure proper escaping in JSON.\n\nWe'll write the code as a string, escaping newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\".\n\nLet's construct:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case['payload']\n    segments = payload['segments']\n    budget = payload['budget']\n    # Ensure we don't mutate case\n    alloc = {}\n    total_min = 0\n    # First pass: alloc minimums\n    for seg in segments:\n        name = seg['name']\n        min_val = seg['minimum']\n        alloc[name] = min_val\n        total_min += min_val\n    remaining = budget - total_min\n    if remaining < 0:\n        # Not enough budget for minimums; allocate proportionally? But assume feasible.\n        # For safety, set remaining = 0 and reduce allocations? Not specified. We'll just ignore.\n        pass\n    # Create list of (importance, index, name, capacity, min_val) for sorting\n    seg_info = [(seg['importance'], i, seg['name'], seg['capacity'], seg['minimum']) for i, seg in enumerate(segments)]\n    seg_info.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[0], x[1]))  # descending importance, stable by index\n    for imp, idx, name, cap, min_val in seg_info:\n        if remaining <= 0:\n            break\n        available = cap - alloc[name]  # or cap - min_val, same\n        to_add = min(available, remaining)\n     